      "content": "Georgia: The moderate political landscape largely revolves around the Georgian Dream and United National Movement parties. While Georgia Dream, established in 2008, is more centrist and has a pro-European Union stance, it has been marked by internal divisions and hasn't been without controversy. In contrast, the United National Movement, founded in 2003, has a more conservative approach, focusing on national traditions and more traditional values, although it has sought to moderate and handle political dissent peacefully in recent years.\n\nAzerbaijan: A more complex picture due to the People's Front and Unity political movements. The People's Front, dating back to the presidential elections of October 2013, is generally considered a stabilizing force with pro-US and pro-European Union tendencies. It is led by Prime Minister Artur Rasizade and promotes a pro-European agenda, seeking to strengthen economic ties and democratic reforms. The Unity, a smaller party also established in 2013, focuses on traditional values and national identity and is less prominent in capturing the political narrative but seen as moderate compared to other regional groups. Be mindful that while these parties represent more moderate stances, international dynamics related to Azerbaijani policies towards neighboring Armenia and involvement in regional conflicts can add layers of complexity to any interaction",
